Z||000007||DRH||06-28-2001||12:24 AM||||Check your clutch pedal. If it's bent it may be keeping the clutch from engaging. Might cause the starting problem too. There is a safety switch mounted just above the clutch pedal that keeps the car from starting unless the clutch is pushed in. Get under the dash and see what you can see...||||reg||3
Z||000008||fierobaby||06-28-2001||12:52 AM||||dude to bad you live in freakin FL I would be all over that thing for at least a parts car...you got a sunroof? how about selling me the seal!?<P>------------------<BR>1984 Sweet Candy Apple red Fiero sport coupe :-D<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cfiero.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tigger||06-28-2001||01:23 AM||||Sorry hard to tell the problem without looking at it.  Tranny sounds fine, clutch isn't disengaging.  May want to fix the rod on the slave cylinder.<P>Weird how I remember these things.  I was in Florida a year ago.  Visited relatives in Niceville.  Just happened to see a black coupe parked in a lot on the way to Fort Walton Beach airport.  Almost bet now it was yours, cool.||||reg||1
Z||000010||walsh2001||06-28-2001||10:23 AM||||Yeah... it was a great car... I drove it everywhere. The sunroof was a an aftermarket custom job, so it is a little bit smaller than the original. <BR>||||reg||9
Z||000011||SteveJ||06-28-2001||10:56 AM||||It is possible but unlikely that it's the transmission. Hate to see you give up on a good car ( and then have somebody else fix it on the cheap )<BR>Pay attention to DRH's post about the clutch pedal and when you get that correct, work on the master and slave cylinder for the clutch. They sell steel clutch pedals to replace the flimsy original equipment one and there is lots of information here on the forum on the hydraulic clutch.||||reg||1
